  "id": "listening",
  "title": "Listening and pronunciation",
  "priority": 1,
  "order": 11,
  "explanation": "Your biggest practical gap is native-speed listening, so this unit is mostly ears. Every item here plays at the speed set in Settings (0.8, 1 or 1.25, or the native toggle), and the dictation drills hide the text until you have typed what you heard. Use the shadowing mode to hear, repeat and move on.\n\n**Word boundaries.** French runs words together, and casual speech drops sounds. What you hear is often shorter than what is written: je ne sais pas becomes *j'sais pas*, il y a becomes *y a*, tu es becomes *t'es*, je suis becomes *j'suis*. The dictation drills pair the full form with the spoken one; typing either counts.\n\n**Liaison.** A normally silent final consonant is pronounced when the next word starts with a vowel. The s in les becomes a z: les amis is \"lay-za-mi\", les gens ont is \"lay zhahn zon\" (the sheet said an n sound; it is a z). On est is \"on-nay\", c'est un is \"say-tun\".\n\n**Silent final consonants.** Fait is said \"fè\"; the t is only heard in the feminine faite. The same goes for petit / petite, grand / grande, français / française.\n\n**The French R** comes from the back of the throat, like a soft gargle, never from the tongue tip. Shadow the short word lists: le roi, rester, rouge, la rue, Paris, très, trois, la route, rire.\n\n**Telling forms apart by ear.** Some pairs sound close and change the meaning: je voulais (imparfait) vs j'ai voulu (passé composé); mieux vs meilleur; fait vs faite; ce vs se; du vs de. The \"which did you hear?\" drills play one and you pick.\n\n**Numbers** at native speed are a listening problem more than a vocabulary one. Type the digits you hear.",
